WS73 Wi-Fi interface audit
=========================

From the kernel worktree:

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/wifi/check-abi.py

Requires a clean committed worktree, sibling ws73_SDK checkout, Clang and
llvm-objcopy. Ten pinned SDK headers/source files must byte-match commit d5e68a58fe38c2b04a16769c69de1e8a80e86d74.
The tool compiles declarations and sentinel initializers sequentially for
x86-64, i386, arm64, RISC-V32 and big-endian PowerPC64. It extracts object
sections and checks exact bytes and sizes, plus ELF class/endianness/machine
identity and relocatable-object type. It does not execute the objects,
build/load a driver, open USB, or change the allocated hardware's state.

In addition to FRW/control envelopes, the probe extracts the pinned RX info,
status/statistic and TX-status declarations. HAL pack(1) scopes are preserved;
the outer DMAC declarations stay unpacked. Independent sentinel bytes and
offset/size assertions cover the 32-byte RX control and eight-byte TX status.
The TX probe additionally preserves pack(1) for mac_tx_ctl and its Host-only
expanded suffix. Five byte-sized scalar aliases are extracted from their
pinned declarations; the frame pointee is opaque, without changing pointer
width/alignment. An independent bit-group oracle checks every byte, including
nonzero reserved fields and a pointer sentinel. Offsets prove the copied prefix
ends at 32 and the whole structure is 40 bytes on 32-bit or 44 on 64-bit.
The pinned sender copies OAL_MAX_CB_LEN=32, not sizeof(mac_tx_ctl). Static
source checks are drift guards, not proof of device consumption. No pointer
sentinel or synthetic descriptor is transmitted. The sentinels are deliberately
not valid packets or descriptor statuses.

Records live in the private sibling .dev/ws73-upstream/records directory.
They include SDK excerpts, objects, tool/source hashes and expected/observed
bytes. Do not publish those SDK excerpts or treat the sentinel initializers
as device commands. The repository contains only the independent probe tool.
The first local attempt failed in ccache before compilation; disabling the
cache resolved that environmental failure. All attempts remain recorded.

Passing means these pinned declarations have the expected layout under the
recorded Clang targets. It does not establish the compiler/options used to
build SDK110 firmware, a device producer's layout, valid FRW message values,
real USB transfers, STA support or big-endian driver qualification. The test
deliberately shows why directly copying native pointer structs or bitfields
is unsuitable for a portable byte-oriented codec.

See Documentation/driver-api/ws73-wifi-interface.rst for the source call
chains, configuration/data length differences and remaining implementation
dependencies, including the Host key/register transaction error paths.
No Wi-Fi hardware capability is enabled by this tool.

Bounded FRW codec VM checks
--------------------------

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/wifi/run-kunit.py

Reserve four build jobs and two VM CPUs after checking the shared machine
budget. Requires the same Clang/LLD, make, Python and QEMU/KVM tools as the
other WS73 VM fixtures. The fixed output directory is build-wifi-kunit under
the sibling .dev/ws73-upstream directory, guarded by the shared build lock.
No physical USB, disk or network is assigned to the test VM. Do not run
experimental kernels/modules on the host.

The codec lives at drivers/net/wireless/hisilicon/ws73/frw.c and currently
builds only with WS73_WIFI_KUNIT_TEST. It exports no public symbols and
registers no adapter or auxiliary/USB consumer. This is an independently
implemented candidate byte codec for the pinned SDK110 control representation;
original-firmware request/reply behavior remains unqualified.

The encoder produces an eight-byte FRW extension plus a 28-byte communication
envelope and request body, with explicit little-endian values and zero device
address slots. Both synchronous flags are set. The decoder accepts only the
plain synchronous RSP variant, requiring consistent ID/VAP across both headers,
expected ID/VAP/sequence, exact envelope/body lengths and reply length bounded
by BOTH the received capacity and the caller's actual capacity. It never clips
an oversized reply. It preserves signed device_status separately from parser
success. Remote addresses, returned request data_len and nested flags do not
become pointers or override validation. Caller retains borrowed reply storage.

Both request and reply payloads have a local 1024-byte bound; this is not a
firmware maximum or device credit. The APIs operate on an exact unpadded FRW
record, not HCC/USB framing. The eventual transport must validate/remove outer
padding before parsing and add initialized padding for TX. Caller-owned inputs
and outputs must be retained and non-overlapping. Error leaves output unchanged.

Eight KUnit cases check independent request/reply vectors, all 40 truncated
reply extents in exact-sized unaligned allocations, NULL/size_t extremes,
empty/maximum payloads, foreign ID/VAP/sequence, mismatched headers/roles,
unsupported extension flags, length/capacity disagreement, immutable error
outputs, signed status values and hostile remote address slots. Tests run with
SMP, KASAN, UBSAN, lockdep and DEBUG_ATOMIC_SLEEP. The supervisor requires the
exact case set in JSON AND ordered raw KTAP, totals, suite completion, VM reboot,
requested config and zero tool exit, and rejects kernel diagnostics. Source,
config, tools, compiled codec/test objects, image and raw logs are archived.

The codec itself does not implement transaction state; the component below adds
that local contract. Device epoch validation, command whitelist, HCC routing,
key transactions, mac80211 operations and functional STA still need the real
consumer and original-firmware validation. A tuple match alone
cannot prove freshness after reconnect; parser success is not command success.

Bounded FRW transactions
------------------------
The Wi-Fi test module also links frw-transaction.c. One sleepable caller owns
command storage, while an independent atomic-safe RX entry point validates and
copies a bounded reply. A second caller gets EBUSY. Sequences start at one and
are never reused in an instance; exhaustion permanently closes admission.
The timeout is one absolute monotonic deadline (at most 2000 ms), including send.
The transport must honor that deadline and drain borrowed command storage before
returning. Exact local send length and an accepted reply are both required.
Device callback status is returned separately, including nonzero status.

Timeout, short/failed send, malformed active reply, duplicate accepted reply or
abort closes the instance. Old tuples cannot complete a subsequent command.
Shutdown aborts and joins the admitted caller; the owner still has to cancel its
transport and stop/join RX and every other entry point before freeing storage.
Already admitted send work can overlap abort; the transport must enforce its own
disconnect admission and cancellation. RX never takes the command mutex;
the state spinlock is released before transport.send and reply waiting.
Inputs/result must not alias the transaction's storage or each other.

The nine transaction cases add early reply with failed/short/long send, duplicate
and malformed replies, abort, expired and missing replies, timely reply with expired send, sequence exhaustion,
invalid requests, copied RX bytes, concurrent admission and real kthread shutdown
draining. Together with the eight codec cases these are 17 FRW cases; the five RX cases
below bring the current total to 22.
The runner requires both suites, every ordered case, raw summaries and VM exit.

This is an independently testable internal component, not an active Wi-Fi USB
consumer. No command whitelist, register access, mac80211 adapter, firmware ABI
qualification or device epoch/reset guarantee follows from these tests. The
candidate codec's exact response profile still requires original-firmware
observation. A new local object alone cannot justify retrying uncertain hardware.

Bounded receive frame spans
---------------------------
rx-frame.c decodes the candidate non-WS53 record AFTER HCC/FRW validation and
removal: 32-byte CB, 36-byte reserved MAC-header area, then frame body. It
accepts only non-AMSDU, one-buffer storage, with an initial local 4096-byte
allocation bound and at most 31 trailing DMA padding bytes. Those restrictions
are local policy, not established SDK110 firmware limits or advertised MTU.

Before returning immutable borrowed spans it checks the header reservation,
frame length versus header length and actual received body capacity. Device
header addresses are ignored. No in-place move or skb length update occurs;
a future consumer can copy precisely the validated header and body. Cipher and
status nibbles remain raw values, with no FCS/decryption/replay claim. 802.11
header validity, FRW identity/length conventions, VAP/epoch and original firmware
export still require validation before mac80211 delivery. Raw channel/RSSI are
not a supported-channel or PHY declaration.

Five RX cases cover an independent unaligned vector with a hostile device
pointer and poisoned unused header space, every truncation in exact-sized
allocations, all six-bit header lengths, every allowed padding extent,
underflow/overflow, unsupported split/AMSDU storage, recovery after rejection,
maximum local allocation, SIZE_MAX and NULL. There are now 22 cases in three
suites. The strict runner requires their order and archives both new objects.
No hardware or wireless interface is registered by these tests.
